        "label": "Least value of y",
        "accept": "$-16$, at $x = 1$",
        "wrong": [
          {
            "id": "intercept",
            "value": -15,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "x-value",
            "value": 1,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "sign",
            "value": 16,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "$-16$, at $x = 1$.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Go halfway between the roots, then work out the height.",
        "byKey": {
          "intercept": "$-15$ is the $y$-intercept. The lowest point is at $x = 1$.",
          "x-value": "1 is where the lowest point is. Give its height.",
          "sign": "$4 \\times (-4) = -16$: the vertex is below the axis."
        }
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "The roots are $-3$ and 5, so the vertex is at $x = 1$. There $y = 4 \\times (-4) = -16$."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r3-intercept",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "skills": [
        "quad-graph"
      ],
      "prose": "A curve is $y = 2(x - 1)(x + 4)$.",
      "question": "Where does it cross the $y$-axis?",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "numeric",
        "answer": -8,
        "tol": {
          "abs": 0
        },
        "label": "y-intercept",
        "accept": "$-8$: $2 \\times (-1) \\times 4$",
        "wrong": [
          {
            "id": "vertex",
            "value": -12.5,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0.005
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "no-a",
            "value": -4,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "sign",
            "value": 8,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "$2 \\times (-1) \\times 4 = -8$.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Put $x = 0$ into the equation.",
        "byKey": {
          "vertex": "$-12.5$ is the vertex height, at $x = -1.5$. The intercept is at $x = 0$.",
          "no-a": "You left out the 2 in front.",
          "sign": "$-1 \\times 4$ is negative."
        }
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "At $x = 0$ the brackets are $-1$ and 4. The product is $-4$, and the 2 makes it $-8$."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r3-outside",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "skills": [
        "quad-solve"
      ],
      "prose": "Solve $x^2 - x - 12 > 0$.",
      "question": "Which is the solution?",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "choice",
        "layout": "grid",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "$x < -3$ or $x > 4$",
            "correct": true
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "$-3 < x < 4$",
            "why": "That is where the curve is below the axis. $> 0$ asks for above."
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "$x < 3$ or $x > -4$",
            "why": "You took the roots from the wrong signs. $(x - 4)(x + 3)$ has roots 4 and $-3$."
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "text": "$x > 4$",
            "why": "You kept one side. $x = -5$ also works: $25 + 5 - 12 = 18$."
          }
        ],
        "shuffle": false
      },
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "Outside the roots $-3$ and 4.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Factorise, then ask where the curve is above the axis."
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "$x^2 - x - 12 = (x - 4)(x + 3)$, with roots $-3$ and 4. The curve opens upwards, so it is above the axis outside the roots."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r3-line-meets",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "skills": [
        "quad-solve",
        "quad-graph"
      ],
      "prose": "The line $y = 2x + 3$ crosses the parabola $y = x^2$ twice.",
      "canvas": {
        "kind": "plane",
        "params": {
          "x": [
            -3,
            5
          ],
          "y": [
            -2,
            12
          ],
          "grid": true,
          "height": 320,
          "functions": [
            {
              "id": "f",
              "expr": "x^2"
            },
            {
              "id": "g",
              "expr": "2*x + 3",
              "colour": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "state": {}
      },
      "question": "What is the height of the right-hand crossing?",
      "widget": {
        "kind": "numeric",
        "answer": 9,
        "tol": {
          "abs": 0
        },
        "label": "Height",
        "accept": "9, at $(3, 9)$",
        "wrong": [
          {
            "id": "x-value",
            "value": 3,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "left",
            "value": 1,
            "tol": {
              "abs": 0
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "feedback": {
        "correct": "$(3, 9)$: $3^2 = 9 = 2 \\times 3 + 3$.",
        "generic": "Not quite right. Solve $x^2 = 2x + 3$, then find the height.",
        "byKey": {
          "x-value": "3 is where the crossing is. Give its height.",
          "left": "1 is the height of the left crossing, at $x = -1$."
        }
      },
      "explain": {
        "text": "$x^2 = 2x + 3$ gives $(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$. So $x = 3$ or $-1$. The right crossing is $(3, 9)$."
      }
    },
